{"reply":"- **Anonymity:** even the researcher can't link responses to a person (e.g. no names collected).
- **Confidentiality:** the researcher knows who participated but protects that information.

Good practice: use **ID numbers instead of names**, store consent forms separately from data, password-protect files, limit access, and report only grouped results. Ghana's **Data Protection Act, 2012 (Act 843)** governs personal data.","action":null}

{"reply":"**Ordinal logistic regression** (the proportional odds model) handles ordered outcomes such as poor / fair / good. It models the odds of being at or below each category versus above it.

The **proportional odds assumption** says each predictor has the same odds ratio at every cut-point: the effect of age on being 'fair or better' versus 'poor' equals its effect on 'good' versus 'fair or poor'. This gives one odds ratio per predictor.

If the assumption fails for a predictor, its single odds ratio misrepresents the data. Options include a partial proportional odds model, a multinomial model (which ignores the ordering), or collapsing the outcome into a binary one when that is scientifically sensible. QuantAI assumes proportional odds rather than testing it; the exported Stata code (brant test) or R code can check it.","action":null}
